@node Overview
@chapter Overview

@code{bocl} runs an interactive read-eval-print loop, and can load files and
evaluate forms given on the command line before optionally entering the REPL.
On start-up it loads its system Lisp files --- the prelude and its supporting
files (@file{cl-exports.lisp}, @file{bignum.lisp},
@file{bignum-integration.lisp}).

@node Invocation
@chapter Invocation

@example
bocl [options]
@end example

The most common options are summarized below; see @command{bocl(1)} for the
full list.

@table @option
@item -h, --help
Print a usage summary and exit.
@item -V, --version
Print @code{(LISP-IMPLEMENTATION-VERSION)} and exit.
@item -l, --load @var{file}
Load @var{file}.
@item -x, -e, --eval @var{form}
Evaluate @var{form} (quote it for the shell).
@item -p, --package @var{package}
Start in @var{package}.
@item -b, --batch
Exit on end of file on @code{*STANDARD-INPUT*}.
@item -n, -norc, --no-init
Do not load @file{~/.boclrc}.
@item --no-prelude
Bare kernel only --- do not load the prelude.
@item --on-error @var{action}
What to do when an unhandled error reaches the top level: one of @code{exit}
(the default), @code{debug}, @code{abort}, @code{appease}.  @xref{Error handling}.
@item -quit, --quit
Quit when argument processing is done (otherwise enter the REPL).
@end table

@node Error handling
@chapter Error handling

The @option{--on-error} option (legacy alias @option{-on-error}) selects what bocl
does when an @emph{unhandled} @code{SERIOUS-CONDITION} reaches the top level --- an
error your own code did not catch with @code{HANDLER-CASE}, @code{IGNORE-ERRORS}, or
a restart.  The action is published to Lisp as @code{BOCL:*ON-ERROR*}.  The default
is @code{exit}.

@table @code
@item exit
@emph{At the REPL:} report the condition and exit the process (status 1).
@emph{While loading a file:} the error aborts the load and the process exits.
This is the fail-hard mode for batch use, scripts, and CI.
@item debug
Enter the interactive debugger (@code{INVOKE-DEBUGGER}): condition banner, backtrace,
restart menu, and a debug REPL.  If the debugger is continued, control returns to the
prompt.  The natural choice for interactive development.  @xref{Debugger}.
@item abort
Report the condition and return to the top-level REPL --- no debugger, the process
stays alive.  While loading a file, the failing form aborts the whole load.
@item appease
The same as @code{abort} at the REPL.  While loading a file, however, a failing
top-level form is reported (@samp{;; [load] skipped a form: @dots{}}) and skipped, and
loading continues to the end (skips are counted in @code{*LOAD-ERROR-COUNT*}).  This
is meant for loading large or partially-supported suites (e.g.@: the SACLA / ANSI
tests) so they reach their final report instead of dying on the first unsupported
form.
@end table

So: @code{exit} fails hard; @code{debug} opens the debugger; @code{abort} returns to
the REPL; and @code{appease} differs from @code{abort} only for @code{LOAD}, where it
skips bad forms and keeps going.  (The @code{BOCL:*ON-ERROR*} variable itself defaults
to @code{:DEBUG}, but the launcher overrides it from @option{--on-error} unless
@option{--no-prelude} is given.)

@node Files
@chapter Files

The default install prefix is @file{/opt/local}.

@table @file
@item @var{PREFIX}/bin/bocl
The executable.
@item @var{PREFIX}/libexec/bocl/
The system Lisp files loaded at start-up: @file{prelude.lisp},
@file{cl-exports.lisp}, @file{bignum.lisp}, @file{bignum-integration.lisp}.
@item @var{PREFIX}/lib/libbocl.*
The runtime library (static and shared) for embedding bocl in a C program.
@item @var{PREFIX}/include/bocl/
The C headers for embedding.
@item ~/.boclrc
Per-user init file (unless @option{--no-init}).
@end table

The environment variable @env{BOCL_PRELUDE}, if set to a readable path,
overrides the prelude search.

@node Debugger
@chapter The minimal debugger

When an unhandled @code{SERIOUS-CONDITION} reaches the top level under
@option{--on-error debug} (or via @code{INVOKE-DEBUGGER} / @code{BREAK}), bocl
enters a minimal debugger: it prints the condition, a backtrace and the
available restarts, then runs a read-eval-print loop.  Keyword commands
(@code{:backtrace}, @code{:restart}, @code{:continue}, @code{:abort},
@code{:quit}, @code{:help}) and bare restart numbers select actions; any other
form is evaluated as Lisp in the error's dynamic context.  The complete
specification is in @file{debugger-specifications.org}.

